  "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement I is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(0)=1,\n$$\n\n \nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ Therefore, it's not differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
